  'This course studies American constitutional rights and civil liberties through Supreme Court doctrine, constitutional text, historical development, litigation strategy, and contemporary public-law disputes. Students examine due process, equal protection, speech, press, religion, criminal procedure, privacy, voting, state action, incorporation, federalism, remedies, and current civil-liberties conflicts involving technology, education, national security, public health, and democracy.',
  $clcljson${"slug": "constitutional-law-civil-liberties", "title": "Constitutional Law / Civil Liberties", "description": "This course studies American constitutional rights and civil liberties through Supreme Court doctrine, constitutional text, historical development, litigation strategy, and contemporary public-law disputes. Students examine due process, equal protection, speech, press, religion, criminal procedure, privacy, voting, state action, incorporation, federalism, remedies, and current civil-liberties conflicts involving technology, education, national security, public health, and democracy.", "topics": [{"id": "14f6f71a-8794-4e34-aea6-839ae6b260cf", "type": "topic", "title": "Constitutional Foundations, Civil Liberties, and Interpretive Method", "description": "Frame American civil liberties as rights claims made through text, history, doctrine, structure, precedent, institutions, and social movements.", "children": [{"id": "0a3ed85e-ac4f-4c7e-99a7-f96f8c77da37", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Constitutional text and rights architecture", "children": [{"id": "f5b023a0-d847-4129-bef1-342e66b6de5d", "type": "concept", "title": "Articles, Bill of Rights, Reconstruction Amendments, and later amendments"}, {"id": "69d86019-86ee-48d4-ae86-df95f9282661", "type": "concept", "title": "Negative liberties, equality guarantees, procedural protections, and structural safeguards"}, {"id": "560a1c61-1d8c-4e6e-8ba5-49fa1b1b08ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil liberties, civil rights, individual rights, group rights, and institutional power"}], "description": "Students learn where rights come from and how the Constitution organizes liberty claims."}, {"id": "74cda9af-73d6-408a-929d-38f4b99fde43", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modes of interpretation", "children": [{"id": "1b0b8075-929a-4485-b264-dd6a0b7dd907", "type": "concept", "title": "Textualism, originalism, living constitutionalism, common-law constitutionalism, and representation-reinforcement"}, {"id": "5052b9ac-5277-4dbc-956b-fbf63a875c80", "type": "concept", "title": "History and tradition, precedent, consequences, constitutional structure, and moral reasoning"}, {"id": "103ffa28-7aee-44b7-89d0-4d4cdf409b9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Majority, concurrence, dissent, dicta, holding, rule, standard, and test"}], "description": "Interpretive method shapes every disputed civil-liberties case."}, {"id": "a4e12423-6631-43f8-92e4-28227f2d8bb1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Civil liberties in American political development", "children": [{"id": "0784e88e-5b96-4eae-b570-f96330b3b217", "type": "concept", "title": "Slavery, Reconstruction, Jim Crow, incorporation, civil rights movements, and modern polarization"}, {"id": "c0bc396b-de2c-4716-8178-2ee00bb02ef2", "type": "concept", "title": "Federal courts, Congress, states, social movements, agencies, and public opinion"}, {"id": "05644d55-536a-4251-aa56-b21dd25a9226", "type": "concept", "title": "Rights expansion, backlash, enforcement gaps, and contested constitutional memory"}], "description": "Rights doctrine becomes clearer when students see it as part of history and power."}]}, {"id": "f01078b0-a2a4-4e3c-918e-d1d9be46ea5c", "type": "topic", "title": "Judicial Review, Justiciability, Remedies, and Constitutional Litigation", "description": "Teach how constitutional claims reach courts and what courts can actually do.", "children": [{"id": "666c0667-585b-40f4-94ec-1023b76a3ee6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Judicial review and constitutional supremacy", "children": [{"id": "322f4781-124b-4c2e-85b3-db6e82dbc054", "type": "concept", "title": "Marbury v. Madison, departmentalism, judicial supremacy, and constitutional dialogue"}, {"id": "b381d459-ca43-46b5-a887-8f67697c8335", "type": "concept", "title": "Supreme Court hierarchy, lower federal courts, state courts, and precedent"}, {"id": "5b68e619-fd90-4f9a-afef-2e1b4e94d205", "type": "concept", "title": "Countermajoritarian difficulty, legitimacy, and democratic accountability"}], "description": "Students learn why courts can invalidate government action and why that power is contested."}, {"id": "ef2c5099-203b-4ba8-84ce-2af763b476c0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Justiciability and access to courts", "children": [{"id": "00b42665-eefc-4231-a199-9ca5c7588222", "type": "concept", "title": "Standing, injury, causation, redressability, ripeness, mootness, political questions, and advisory opinions"}, {"id": "2f884f21-361b-464e-8bb2-9f71ffebfc53", "type": "concept", "title": "Third-party standing, taxpayer standing, organizational standing, and overbreadth exceptions"}, {"id": "9f52878c-96a2-464c-9f99-04e401d577c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Emergency docket, equitable discretion, class actions, and institutional plaintiffs"}], "description": "Rights depend on doctrines that decide who may sue, when, and over what injury."}, {"id": "7c069e33-763d-419f-bfcc-1740054905e3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Remedies and rights enforcement", "children": [{"id": "fcd4b30d-4ad9-4758-b36b-04abe35ebf23", "type": "concept", "title": "Injunctions, declaratory relief, damages, habeas, suppression, exclusion, and structural reform"}, {"id": "9ed3adf9-2ed5-4642-baec-f0b8141c841e", "type": "concept", "title": "Section 1983, Bivens, qualified immunity, sovereign immunity, and municipal liability"}, {"id": "0c34e5e2-5085-4286-a694-511414823c58", "type": "concept", "title": "Facial versus as-applied challenges, severability, nationwide injunctions, and remedial tailoring"}], "description": "Constitutional law is practical only if students understand remedies."}]}, {"id": "115bb4f5-fdd3-4a9a-b689-ba93cc274d05", "type": "topic", "title": "Federalism, Separation of Powers, State Action, and Incorporation Context", "description": "Give civil-liberties students the structural context that determines who is bound by constitutional rights.", "children": [{"id": "38b18c9a-0b2a-4732-b149-b78ef4a1916c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Federalism and rights", "children": [{"id": "b1778087-d7aa-4881-8968-fd313a7c3b0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Enumerated powers, Tenth Amendment, Supremacy Clause, preemption, and anti-commandeering"}, {"id": "45720bbd-a6a5-424f-907d-c3b3ce210f7a", "type": "concept", "title": "Fourteenth Amendment enforcement power and Reconstruction constitutionalism"}, {"id": "fc0b040c-bd94-4886-98f7-9efea42513c1", "type": "concept", "title": "State constitutions, state courts, local government, and rights laboratories"}], "description": "Federalism can protect liberty, frustrate rights enforcement, or create rights variation."}, {"id": "a1fd8792-586f-44b0-a4ef-7c821134b8fa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Separation of powers and emergencies", "children": [{"id": "79e375a3-c15a-42e1-a32f-c39dace2177b", "type": "concept", "title": "Congressional power, executive power, war powers, Youngstown framework, and administrative authority"}, {"id": "85b4f6a6-fd03-455e-ac5f-53b902bceb46", "type": "concept", "title": "National security, immigration, emergency powers, public health, and emergency litigation"}, {"id": "85f32474-146e-4ee9-ab8a-0b25d20aa7bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Judicial deference, political accountability, and civil-liberties risk"}], "description": "Rights disputes often arise when branches claim urgent power."}, {"id": "75427a08-d31c-4ed7-a852-e708da32ddaa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State action and incorporation", "children": [{"id": "6c4a34e4-f184-4147-882c-b26f1a5fcbe5", "type": "concept", "title": "State action doctrine, public function, entanglement, coercion, encouragement, and joint participation"}, {"id": "3f83e4e0-bd81-4f90-8d39-2bedcebe3fa5", "type": "concept", "title": "Fourteenth Amendment incorporation, selective incorporation, and ordered liberty"}, {"id": "2f7d60ba-57c6-4382-ae20-2ab9609f16d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil Rights Cases, Shelley, public accommodations, platforms, and privatized governance"}], "description": "Students learn when private conduct becomes constitutional conduct and which rights bind states."}]}, {"id": "ea8609cd-75b2-41d2-abc6-20690b9e8b0b", "type": "topic", "title": "Bill of Rights Incorporation and Ordered Liberty", "description": "Study how rights first written against the federal government became enforceable against states.", "children": [{"id": "930eb5e2-2b49-4121-aa9a-ef7a51e3e67a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Incorporation doctrine", "children": [{"id": "59907518-1ae0-4662-b225-abf41e899ceb", "type": "concept", "title": "Privileges or Immunities Clause, Due Process Clause, and selective incorporation"}, {"id": "7759bf2e-0ee7-40ad-80f8-6f9e1118115b", "type": "concept", "title": "Fundamental-to-ordered-liberty test and deeply rooted rights"}, {"id": "bf151023-593c-41bc-83e3-258ef7819f40", "type": "concept", "title": "McDonald, Timbs, jury rights, criminal procedure, and remaining unincorporated rights"}], "description": "Incorporation explains why most rights litigation is against state and local government."}, {"id": "bcfc2bfa-0c36-456c-bb0e-f1650298c524", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ordered liberty and rights hierarchy", "children": [{"id": "71c4cf51-5b2e-4ce0-9613-8c76773c9165", "type": "concept", "title": "Fundamental rights, preferred freedoms, rational basis, intermediate scrutiny, strict scrutiny, and history-and-tradition tests"}, {"id": "7bc8d8c8-cb7b-44cc-81d1-8a315536fed9", "type": "concept", "title": "Rights balancing, categorical rules, tiers of scrutiny, and administrability"}, {"id": "96c55df1-9f13-4988-8b2b-9c1bc60ba0d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional competence, judicial restraint, and judicial engagement"}], "description": "Students learn that not all rights receive the same doctrinal treatment."}, {"id": "4bb24861-3e8f-4e9e-82b4-953e5984b0d8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rights against states and local officials", "children": [{"id": "99094d13-3830-48e9-a384-d2eee20de31c", "type": "concept", "title": "Municipal ordinances, school boards, police departments, prisons, and licensing bodies"}, {"id": "78a092b3-5fd2-4779-82e0-6149a50c5b3c", "type": "concept", "title": "State constitutional analogues and independent state grounds"}, {"id": "edb3affd-eeac-4743-8165-5b97ec9ddeae", "type": "concept", "title": "Federalism, uniformity, and local democratic experimentation"}], "description": "Incorporation doctrine becomes concrete through police, schools, prisons, zoning, and local regulation."}]}, {"id": "f5b94a2e-98f6-467b-a2bc-a555ab723b88", "type": "topic", "title": "Procedural Due Process, Fair Procedures, and Government Deprivation", "description": "Learn how the Constitution regulates government procedures before life, liberty, or property is taken.", "children": [{"id": "b1d7d5e0-0a00-477a-86d6-ee8da281b842", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Protected interests", "children": [{"id": "d3714813-e38d-4a77-8acd-76647c97ca2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Property interests, entitlement theory, licenses, employment, benefits, and education"}, {"id": "c231e9ab-8ab5-400a-b81d-d96f115612b1", "type": "concept", "title": "Liberty interests, stigma-plus, physical restraint, reputation, and family integrity"}, {"id": "4bd1e103-7ac0-4cf7-a75b-e43c2ba7ff9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Prison, immigration, parole, discipline, and administrative deprivations"}], "description": "Due process begins by identifying whether life, liberty, or property is at stake."}, {"id": "a8baa979-e3f4-4a88-ab7d-8162ae973063", "type": "subtopic", "title": "What process is due", "children": [{"id": "4f016489-b505-42ab-8740-92113895c7bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Mathews v. Eldridge factors: private interest, risk of error, value of safeguards, and government burden"}, {"id": "a8b439f6-33bd-419b-99f7-0ac1b6846618", "type": "concept", "title": "Notice, hearing, neutral decisionmaker, counsel, evidence, confrontation, and written reasons"}, {"id": "47810fc4-4251-46c2-a2f0-b634f085e0db", "type": "concept", "title": "Pre-deprivation versus post-deprivation process and emergency exceptions"}], "description": "Students learn the balancing framework for procedural fairness."}, {"id": "f4acbcf6-986d-4b9e-9eb0-bb9bb47107e7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Administrative and institutional settings", "children": [{"id": "81e25abb-597d-435d-838b-01d26cca1e5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Schools, welfare, disability benefits, public employment, licensing, housing, and civil commitment"}, {"id": "b6e7866f-950d-48bc-b80e-b52706537e0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Campus discipline, immigration proceedings, algorithmic decision-making, and automated benefits"}, {"id": "78ce87aa-61da-4a6f-aafc-1621359d8fa1", "type": "concept", "title": "Remedy design, harmless error, exhaustion, and judicial review"}], "description": "Procedural due process is a practical field of hearings, records, and institutional design."}]}, {"id": "3414a96b-7018-43fe-88e1-15c9f28e98b2", "type": "topic", "title": "Substantive Due Process, Privacy, Family, and Bodily Autonomy", "description": "Study the contested doctrine protecting certain liberties from government interference regardless of procedure.", "children": [{"id": "174a399c-a2f0-4c5e-bb1d-eb67e5277264", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fundamental liberty framework", "children": [{"id": "09ba62f0-becb-4030-a20b-b32668e27c57", "type": "concept", "title": "Deeply rooted tradition, ordered liberty, careful description, and history-and-tradition methodology"}, {"id": "70b4db9d-ba29-4618-9b48-97c239f5ebce", "type": "concept", "title": "Strict scrutiny, rational basis, undue burden, and doctrinal instability"}, {"id": "3a20896f-8428-4379-bb04-fdb1ca7699de", "type": "concept", "title": "Lochner legacy, judicial restraint, and contested legitimacy"}], "description": "Students learn how courts identify and protect unenumerated rights."}, {"id": "91f2bfce-56ef-4413-8fda-1dbc870875fb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Family, marriage, sexuality, and identity", "children": [{"id": "b1414bfc-18d5-4452-9b58-f7086a0ca8cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage, contraception, parenting, family integrity, custody, and education"}, {"id": "902d823c-12ff-4ae0-91cd-78089f200e8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Sexual intimacy, LGBTQ rights, Obergefell, Lawrence, and equal-protection overlap"}, {"id": "9fef57a8-b38c-4267-acf6-d1d0074a8465", "type": "concept", "title": "Adoption, foster care, trans rights, and parental-rights disputes"}], "description": "Substantive due process has shaped intimate life and family governance."}, {"id": "569dd222-49d4-49a2-8140-af33f78d9d08", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Bodily autonomy and reproductive liberty", "children": [{"id": "900c842f-0c73-41f6-84f2-4cb4219120ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Abortion doctrine before and after Dobbs, state constitutional rights, and interstate conflict"}, {"id": "aed8ecbe-5651-4ff4-815a-556399aeb4bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Contraception, pregnancy, medical decision-making, refusal of treatment, and assisted reproduction"}, {"id": "7d671f71-7403-44e7-b7af-79532facb15d", "type": "concept", "title": "Public health, vaccination, bodily integrity, disability, and bioethics"}], "description": "Students confront one of the most contested constitutional fields."}]}, {"id": "7579daa2-f90f-4a46-a615-5859e850a618", "type": "topic", "title": "Equal Protection Framework and Levels of Scrutiny", "description": "Build the analytic core for classification, discrimination, and equality claims.", "children": [{"id": "65a74c59-0da7-4e04-9024-3362decad89b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Equal protection basics", "children": [{"id": "142634fc-a886-42fa-8026-e9fda49a1c44", "type": "concept", "title": "Facial classifications, discriminatory purpose, discriminatory effect, underinclusion, and overinclusion"}, {"id": "2edb9e53-4e56-4083-b92c-8364771e2d9b", "type": "concept", "title": "Rational basis, rational basis with bite, intermediate scrutiny, and strict scrutiny"}, {"id": "3d44205c-508f-453a-97c2-4eb37ec9f64a", "type": "concept", "title": "Fit, tailoring, compelling interests, important interests, and legitimate interests"}], "description": "Students learn how courts identify classifications and choose scrutiny."}, {"id": "2749d137-ec3e-43d9-b036-bfbb351210a0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intent, impact, and proof", "children": [{"id": "042572c2-4b8f-4608-8f21-beba0c7e861a", "type": "concept", "title": "Washington v. Davis, Arlington Heights factors, statistical evidence, history, departures from normal process, and sequence of events"}, {"id": "1e4db15f-e242-487c-b8ff-51dcaed264b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Implicit bias, structural inequality, disparate impact statutes, and constitutional limits"}, {"id": "0f2f4284-92b1-47fc-8f81-833277af825c", "type": "concept", "title": "Burden shifting, comparators, animus, and evidentiary inference"}], "description": "Equality doctrine often turns on evidence of motive and institutional context."}, {"id": "c73667ea-1b9b-4622-aad2-632641d762f2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Remedies and equality enforcement", "children": [{"id": "749da64a-3009-4273-bd79-fa6b3c5cf1cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Injunctions, race-conscious remedies, desegregation orders, consent decrees, and monitoring"}, {"id": "aa2709ae-0567-460a-9825-a62aa2ac1355", "type": "concept", "title": "Leveling up versus leveling down"}, {"id": "1d790a9a-66b7-498c-b03b-fb52fcde4f8e", "type": "concept", "title": "Section 5 enforcement, Title VI, Title VII, Section 1983, and state-law complements"}], "description": "Students learn that equal protection remedies are both doctrinal and institutional."}]}, {"id": "d0376519-1096-4d49-af75-76e8a8725a4d", "type": "topic", "title": "Race, Ethnicity, Voting Rights, and Democratic Equality", "description": "Center race and democratic participation as core constitutional equality fields.", "children": [{"id": "0ae2987f-4c0a-488d-bb57-1407672366a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race and suspect classifications", "children": [{"id": "8da441b9-81d4-4087-898e-bfa3c22aca66", "type": "concept", "title": "Dred Scott, Reconstruction Amendments, Plessy, Brown, desegregation, and modern strict scrutiny"}, {"id": "22a2f232-bfc0-432c-b8c0-e4f08f64d5eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Benign versus invidious classifications, affirmative action, diversity rationales, and remedial interests"}, {"id": "882040f8-7d9f-44c0-bfae-0f76e5718bc1", "type": "concept", "title": "Education, contracting, employment, admissions, and government programs"}], "description": "Students learn why racial classifications receive strict scrutiny and how doctrine treats racial purpose."}, {"id": "4187ddd1-7237-447a-b644-b5d924831034", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Voting rights and representation", "children": [{"id": "396e99ef-a7fe-4a97-954a-2a2f67e32247", "type": "concept", "title": "One person one vote, reapportionment, ballot access, political parties, and election administration"}, {"id": "030d498a-a9a0-4a2d-9f63-4de7b1b3b458", "type": "concept", "title": "Racial gerrymandering, partisan gerrymandering, vote dilution, and Voting Rights Act context"}, {"id": "cee58e12-3e9c-47f4-bd04-77584d0d43b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Mail voting, voter ID, felon disenfranchisement, campaign administration, and emergency election disputes"}], "description": "Political equality is a constitutional and statutory field."}, {"id": "c1ce7888-0af5-4d09-b1d2-9845bc13a9bf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Citizenship, immigration, and belonging", "children": [{"id": "e23946f1-08d0-4e60-a575-c0a8931e6185", "type": "concept", "title": "Alienage classifications, undocumented persons, birthright citizenship, and immigration federalism"}, {"id": "c55e71e7-ab24-48ef-9c20-fa96e748a4d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Language access, tribal sovereignty, equal protection, and plenary power"}, {"id": "78fa7e06-061f-4ca9-a26b-a6fb0a87e7ef", "type": "concept", "title": "National security, exclusion, detention, and constitutional limits at the border"}], "description": "Race and ethnicity intersect with citizenship, alienage, and national membership."}]}, {"id": "fc368417-49b5-4b46-9441-62e9d5a63b18", "type": "topic", "title": "Sex, Gender, Sexual Orientation, Disability, and Emerging Equality Claims", "description": "Extend equality doctrine beyond race while showing how categories and scrutiny evolve.", "children": [{"id": "36f7036d-b085-4123-87f7-715dab808f5a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sex and gender classifications", "children": [{"id": "39d96368-cd5c-489a-9cb5-67cd3791e5f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Frontiero, Craig, Virginia, stereotypes, pregnancy, caregiving, and sex-based generalizations"}, {"id": "158233b5-1bb5-4c93-8592-41a868482ad8", "type": "concept", "title": "Biological difference, military service, education, athletics, and public facilities"}, {"id": "fbe71a9c-94ce-4569-9392-c50e7e6fa61c", "type": "concept", "title": "Equal Rights Amendment history and state constitutional equality provisions"}], "description": "Students learn intermediate scrutiny and its modern applications."}, {"id": "807d4c42-377c-460f-ba3a-f997b8c15f71", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sexual orientation and gender identity", "children": [{"id": "31144485-ba28-4c6c-8726-a64787ca7266", "type": "concept", "title": "Romer, Lawrence, Windsor, Obergefell, Bostock context, and animus doctrine"}, {"id": "68d5049b-145b-4158-8977-ba537ff48d23", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage, adoption, public accommodations, employment, education, and healthcare"}, {"id": "85cb475d-6be2-4632-b60c-f611c5aa6e9f", "type": "concept", "title": "Transgender rights, youth healthcare, sports, speech, religion, and parental claims"}], "description": "Students connect equal protection, due process, statutory rights, and contemporary disputes."}, {"id": "8bd6494d-2edc-4ba7-a333-3165050f4b91", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Disability, poverty, age, and other classifications", "children": [{"id": "d8a035e8-9188-4de3-97dd-771a65035514", "type": "concept", "title": "Disability, age, wealth, education, homelessness, and criminal history"}, {"id": "ba95adee-52a6-4636-b221-d150e81f4fbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Rational basis review, statutory civil-rights overlays, and access-to-justice concerns"}, {"id": "225ee735-8e97-44fe-9a25-0776ecb8f051", "type": "concept", "title": "Equal protection versus due process, administrative burdens, and social citizenship"}], "description": "Not all important classifications receive heightened scrutiny, which raises hard policy questions."}]}, {"id": "0e2102b2-b5db-4131-99c2-e4401e6237d8", "type": "topic", "title": "First Amendment Speech Foundations", "description": "Study the core rules protecting expression from government control.", "children": [{"id": "a2a9cbc0-a424-42be-a06e-e164fd62096e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Why speech is protected", "children": [{"id": "3d5c6be3-5d11-43e0-852e-6f4c3d3c9e9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Democratic self-government, marketplace of ideas, autonomy, truth seeking, and checking function"}, {"id": "99553f1c-04a4-4228-bdf7-7a011762edf8", "type": "concept", "title": "Speech, expression, conduct, association, press, and petition"}, {"id": "59011e17-bdc7-4366-b800-dd6470078979", "type": "concept", "title": "State action, government regulation, private platforms, and public values"}], "description": "Students learn the theories that explain strong protection for expression."}, {"id": "623e84ed-3942-47f3-9c02-eadf13daa2ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Content and viewpoint discrimination", "children": [{"id": "1b4606b5-1de8-449c-9a33-6613ab2f6dae", "type": "concept", "title": "Content-based regulation, viewpoint discrimination, strict scrutiny, and facial neutrality"}, {"id": "433676d2-7b3f-4331-b934-8907b5d76f53", "type": "concept", "title": "Speaker-based distinctions, subject-matter distinctions, compelled speech, and editorial discretion"}, {"id": "23095203-b58d-4ba0-95cc-3e19d1594797", "type": "concept", "title": "Overbreadth, vagueness, prior restraints, and chilling effects"}], "description": "The central First Amendment danger is government control over ideas."}, {"id": "0b683bab-ff88-4799-ad82-725546817537", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Unprotected and less-protected speech", "children": [{"id": "82b34bb3-c7b3-42b3-ab80-d784e542e67e", "type": "concept", "title": "Incitement, true threats, fighting words, obscenity, defamation, fraud, and child sexual abuse material"}, {"id": "5014265a-0702-47ba-a29c-bcba0a42dc18", "type": "concept", "title": "Commercial speech, professional speech, campaign finance, and government-subsidized speech"}, {"id": "90eb7916-cc56-4a06-b17a-59b35f26f25d", "type": "concept", "title": "Categorical rules, historical tests, and balancing approaches"}], "description": "Students learn the narrow categories where speech may be regulated more directly."}]}, {"id": "e86db01c-7a1b-4328-8984-c80acb67451b", "type": "topic", "title": "Speech Regulation: Forums, Time-Place-Manner, Schools, Government Speech, and Platforms", "description": "Apply speech doctrine to public spaces, institutions, and modern communication systems.", "children": [{"id": "5b149e36-62b9-48a8-ab29-f9998e23aa27", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public forum doctrine", "children": [{"id": "237fcc16-ec42-4183-a218-64c00e19afcb", "type": "concept", "title": "Traditional public forums, designated forums, limited forums, and nonpublic forums"}, {"id": "04278861-6441-407e-9b95-e13773556e27", "type": "concept", "title": "Time, place, and manner restrictions, alternative channels, narrow tailoring, and significant interests"}, {"id": "b15e6861-911b-4473-9a97-8cc3d13b2186", "type": "concept", "title": "Permits, protests, sidewalks, parks, campuses, airports, and courthouses"}], "description": "Forum analysis determines how much government can regulate speech on government property."}, {"id": "cac2cce9-0b56-4a53-857d-3289dbe13736", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Institutional speech contexts", "children": [{"id": "72898ffd-309e-41e7-9eaf-9033941fd02d", "type": "concept", "title": "Student speech, curricular speech, school discipline, and campus speech"}, {"id": "15f52633-915d-4a7d-b183-b56d1137ddda", "type": "concept", "title": "Public employees, government contractors, public universities, and academic freedom"}, {"id": "da855ff4-629e-494b-81b2-27af8cfbfbfd", "type": "concept", "title": "Prisons, military, public benefits, and unconstitutional conditions"}], "description": "Speech rights shift across schools, workplaces, prisons, military settings, and government programs."}, {"id": "a69d2ea0-d27b-40a5-90f3-353d0a059afd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital speech and platform governance", "children": [{"id": "0591bf57-664c-443c-92e9-18cf033db707", "type": "concept", "title": "Social media, content moderation, common-carrier theories, jawboning, and state platform laws"}, {"id": "0bac231a-149c-4ac8-b752-8e779c7bc549", "type": "concept", "title": "Government accounts, blocking users, public-official speech, and state action online"}, {"id": "1fc2a083-5f53-43e0-8c37-60edb1a81d6f", "type": "concept", "title": "Algorithms, misinformation, harassment, anonymity, and digital public squares"}], "description": "Modern civil liberties require serious attention to online speech."}]}, {"id": "51b56560-d619-4613-8737-9b84d657fec7", "type": "topic", "title": "Press, Assembly, Association, Petition, and Campaign Finance", "description": "Cover the expressive freedoms that organize democratic participation beyond individual speech.", "children": [{"id": "ce2c3578-7f95-466c-90a4-a22e6056c07d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Freedom of the press and newsgathering", "children": [{"id": "102ec05e-0fcb-4be4-8632-ea4350863c1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Prior restraints, publication of truthful information, leaks, national security, and reporter privilege"}, {"id": "1670a747-fee1-4603-95a1-d426972511a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Defamation, public figures, actual malice, privacy torts, and anti-SLAPP context"}, {"id": "391f8a43-2948-45a5-bfca-f9e9ba0f2f44", "type": "concept", "title": "Press access, recording police, gag orders, and digital journalism"}], "description": "Press doctrine protects publication but leaves hard questions about access and institutional privilege."}, {"id": "dd561b13-6a0e-4f02-afd4-d98455143dd9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Assembly, association, and petition", "children": [{"id": "3f33d400-9055-4391-9212-ff0cfd335a2a", "type": "concept", "title": "NAACP v. Alabama, expressive association, intimate association, compelled association, and disclosure rules"}, {"id": "f933c650-64ab-4c45-bb1f-bde5f193bbb0", "type": "concept", "title": "Protests, marches, boycotts, labor picketing, public safety, and permit systems"}, {"id": "4d95981a-abbe-4061-ba9d-0644017e8921", "type": "concept", "title": "Petition Clause, lobbying, litigation as expression, and retaliation"}], "description": "Collective expression is central to civil-rights movements and democratic contestation."}, {"id": "d337333a-a11d-4e68-ba6e-3e6894e7b8da", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Campaign finance and political participation", "children": [{"id": "95b9578c-c2ed-4a48-9ff7-970ac8b5e882", "type": "concept", "title": "Contribution limits, expenditure limits, independent expenditures, PACs, Super PACs, and disclosure"}, {"id": "c9aac3c9-23cc-46bc-aef9-d3a61bdd3092", "type": "concept", "title": "Buckley, Citizens United, McCutcheon, coordination, public financing, and corruption rationales"}, {"id": "ea853ab6-87df-4966-8c36-224fa876128b", "type": "concept", "title": "Election integrity, foreign influence, digital ads, and democratic equality"}], "description": "Campaign finance doctrine treats money, speech, equality, and corruption as constitutional problems."}]}, {"id": "4e34341a-f63c-4ef9-b41b-caf0cd2a92f3", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion Clauses: Establishment, Free Exercise, and Religious Liberty Conflicts", "description": "Study the constitutional management of religion, state neutrality, and pluralism.", "children": [{"id": "ffa3310b-6a2f-4283-b1b1-670b9018139b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Establishment Clause", "children": [{"id": "43dba36a-1ee1-4397-9398-167126701717", "type": "concept", "title": "Prayer, religious symbols, public funding, schools, monuments, chaplains, and coercion"}, {"id": "810e61e6-2930-4eed-9954-b1aa63784dce", "type": "concept", "title": "Lemon, endorsement, coercion, history-and-tradition, and neutrality"}, {"id": "6b32f84f-b50c-4f8c-8d41-6ad0b5c8fc00", "type": "concept", "title": "Government speech, private speech, and religious expression on public property"}], "description": "Students learn how courts police government support, endorsement, coercion, and historical practices."}, {"id": "257bf0d4-00d8-428b-be26-42e564baf4c5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Free Exercise Clause", "children": [{"id": "2b0fedaf-e520-41b1-aedf-791b25de6875", "type": "concept", "title": "Belief versus conduct, neutral laws of general applicability, Smith, Lukumi, and strict scrutiny"}, {"id": "fd63c03f-d5bd-471b-b1b3-c525a5bd5a89", "type": "concept", "title": "Hybrid rights, individualized exemptions, ministerial exception, and church autonomy"}, {"id": "4c51c6ec-ed73-45d8-9978-5e2cd47cf754", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious schools, employment, prisons, land use, and pandemic restrictions"}], "description": "Free exercise doctrine tests when religious conduct receives constitutional exemption."}, {"id": "e267ce9d-abad-4fe8-9a46-4561d4852d28", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion and equality conflicts", "children": [{"id": "6b42a6c8-2dfb-4d79-be30-b91ae7f5e079", "type": "concept", "title": "Public accommodations, wedding vendors, foster care, employment, and healthcare mandates"}, {"id": "dad576c1-b275-4091-b056-9f2ff9821db6", "type": "concept", "title": "RFRA, RLUIPA, state RFRAs, and statutory exemptions"}, {"id": "2fd3c22a-75e3-434c-9d4e-f0fcf3be1a09", "type": "concept", "title": "Pluralism, dignitary harms, conscience, and institutional settlement"}], "description": "Modern cases often pit religious liberty against antidiscrimination, healthcare, education, and speech claims."}]}, {"id": "a3653864-2bfd-46e2-9346-d526a1611014", "type": "topic", "title": "Criminal Procedure as Civil Liberties: Fourth, Fifth, Sixth, and Eighth Amendments", "description": "Connect civil liberties to policing, prosecution, punishment, and criminal justice institutions.", "children": [{"id": "eccacf1a-ede3-42e1-aa02-ae03ac826b8d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fourth Amendment searches and seizures", "children": [{"id": "485a7e65-6608-41a0-ade0-8e223eca5219", "type": "concept", "title": "Search, seizure, reasonable expectation of privacy, property-based tests, and standing"}, {"id": "baccf6f6-b985-43aa-b4ee-3825a377f9e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Warrants, probable cause, exceptions, stops, frisks, arrests, and reasonableness"}, {"id": "b1e0496e-e6d1-48e2-af23-e8969b3ccc14", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital searches, cell phones, location tracking, drones, databases, and surveillance"}], "description": "Students learn the constitutional baseline for policing and privacy."}, {"id": "1b6ddc8c-7286-4904-88ee-5c3d7e4ecaaf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fifth and Sixth Amendment protections", "children": [{"id": "8d4caf6b-0df8-4d03-9444-454f3d088df9", "type": "concept", "title": "Self-incrimination, Miranda, interrogation, due process voluntariness, and immunity"}, {"id": "12f7443e-4386-41be-98da-56139da83a56", "type": "concept", "title": "Right to counsel, confrontation, compulsory process, jury trial, and speedy trial"}, {"id": "afc49cf9-76f3-4b3e-87a4-de738754d091", "type": "concept", "title": "Plea bargaining, discovery, Brady, effective assistance, and wrongful convictions"}], "description": "Criminal process rights protect against coercion and unfair adjudication."}, {"id": "f230aa31-0ccb-42e5-8eec-e7f77cde4dc0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Eighth Amendment and punishment", "children": [{"id": "a5c75161-ba16-4a90-938a-3b720dba18bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Cruel and unusual punishment, proportionality, death penalty, juvenile sentencing, and prison conditions"}, {"id": "4c8f4b36-e386-485b-94e5-44b4643e3e24", "type": "concept", "title": "Excessive fines, bail, pretrial detention, homelessness enforcement, and civil forfeiture"}, {"id": "1709df72-e0c4-45f1-afdc-0b7d2e833234", "type": "concept", "title": "Qualified immunity, prison litigation, and remedial barriers"}], "description": "Punishment doctrine reveals the Constitution's limits on state violence."}]}, {"id": "8a9d6301-2844-405b-b07d-57ad5eb4c939", "type": "topic", "title": "Second Amendment, Takings, Economic Liberties, and Property-Adjacent Rights", "description": "Treat the rights often connected to public safety, property, markets, and economic regulation.", "children": [{"id": "09e344a9-f859-4ba5-8d7b-11fc0e0ec646", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Second Amendment", "children": [{"id": "06fe1fb7-3e49-498a-8b67-b5a6ab38eeec", "type": "concept", "title": "Heller, McDonald, Bruen, Rahimi, text-history-tradition, and historical analogues"}, {"id": "1747aca8-29f3-4182-aeef-b3910e21771d", "type": "concept", "title": "Public carry, sensitive places, licensing, domestic violence, felons, age restrictions, and assault weapons"}, {"id": "c64b19dd-9316-49c2-8fbf-54d8914cf5b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Federalism, public safety, empirical evidence, and historical method"}], "description": "Students learn the modern shift from militia framing to individual public-carry doctrine."}, {"id": "e0a1676c-1bc6-47c1-a2cd-24307c41f83e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Takings and property rights", "children": [{"id": "f7aa5535-bb06-449b-b85c-498b47c4aa4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Public use, just compensation, physical takings, regulatory takings, and exactions"}, {"id": "72bdbc3c-fc81-422f-85c0-d94ee1bd2cc5", "type": "concept", "title": "Kelo, Loretto, Lucas, Penn Central, Nollan, Dolan, Koontz, and Sheetz"}, {"id": "e4ee6cb8-962a-4a81-ab8a-211be523cd69", "type": "concept", "title": "Housing, land use, environmental regulation, and state constitutional property"}], "description": "Takings doctrine is both constitutional structure and civil-liberties limit on government regulation."}, {"id": "ace0a7e7-d9d7-4f00-a9b1-e821ed1f4c2a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Economic liberties and rational basis", "children": [{"id": "429d7e15-1e4a-4db4-9f28-3caf6dfb6a15", "type": "concept", "title": "Lochner, Nebbia, West Coast Hotel, Carolene Products, and modern rational basis"}, {"id": "1a326bec-3d71-4fd8-adf9-5450b2458261", "type": "concept", "title": "Occupational licensing, contracts, substantive due process, and privileges or immunities"}, {"id": "bc5d4ca3-7ea5-4aa9-b4aa-262189eaa446", "type": "concept", "title": "Economic regulation, inequality, administrative state, and judicial role"}], "description": "Students understand why economic rights moved from Lochner-era protection to deferential review."}]}, {"id": "6dcf65c4-33e5-4a34-a747-08232fd1babb", "type": "topic", "title": "Civil Rights Enforcement, Section 1983, Federal Courts, and Institutional Reform", "description": "Show how rights become enforceable against officials, institutions, and governments.", "children": [{"id": "3874d74e-a0f3-4f08-94b0-de32f629cb80", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Section 1983 litigation", "children": [{"id": "286a0174-4e41-4f91-bf03-3fe45c10b67b", "type": "concept", "title": "Color of law, person, deprivation, causation, municipal liability, and official policy"}, {"id": "6722807f-ef79-457d-814c-b77b6e2b695c", "type": "concept", "title": "Qualified immunity, clearly established law, supervisory liability, and individual-capacity claims"}, {"id": "97b53eaa-f43c-4273-8739-95ec76df4aa5", "type": "concept", "title": "Damages, attorneys' fees, settlement, and litigation strategy"}], "description": "Section 1983 is the practical engine of constitutional civil-rights enforcement."}, {"id": "b4f0e0fe-dacf-4f4b-b0fb-e39847c7ddf7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Federal courts and rights remedies", "children": [{"id": "9dd9844b-b328-4ce7-914d-9adb156a0b51", "type": "concept", "title": "Federal question jurisdiction, removal, abstention, habeas, exhaustion, and Rooker-Feldman"}, {"id": "3b7d1bdd-f5e7-41ef-b6ac-3970e82c4b84", "type": "concept", "title": "Eleventh Amendment, sovereign immunity, Ex parte Young, and state remedies"}, {"id": "cddd8f9d-f3df-496d-bd39-3796f6d9bfdf", "type": "concept", "title": "Class actions, consent decrees, structural injunctions, and institutional reform"}], "description": "Jurisdiction and immunity shape civil-liberties enforcement."}, {"id": "102235e0-98ee-442e-a7ca-3bd53bcdc962", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Statutory civil-rights ecosystem", "children": [{"id": "c7ebcddf-55a6-4edb-8646-13a0614851bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil Rights Act, Voting Rights Act, Title VI, Title VII, Title IX, ADA, RFRA, and RLUIPA"}, {"id": "8bda5302-38d7-406f-9099-27af86f8c089", "type": "concept", "title": "Administrative enforcement, private rights of action, burden shifting, and remedies"}, {"id": "5d616afd-7cef-45fd-86be-9a71338f5150", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersection of constitutional floor and statutory expansion"}], "description": "Constitutional rights are often enforced alongside statutes."}]}, {"id": "a012311f-872d-452f-8b5d-f059a9546a81", "type": "topic", "title": "Contemporary Civil Liberties: Technology, National Security, Public Health, Education, and Democracy", "description": "Bring doctrine into modern disputes where rights, institutions, and technology collide.", "children": [{"id": "a97cd45a-8214-48e8-9b5d-c498c6e15732", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Technology, privacy, and surveillance", "children": [{"id": "39ad2585-8901-4259-ac2f-3b3373141be5", "type": "concept", "title": "Metadata, location tracking, facial recognition, biometrics, AI, predictive policing, and data brokers"}, {"id": "d5aece20-f5b9-4152-8fd8-3feba3badabc", "type": "concept", "title": "Fourth Amendment, First Amendment, due process, equal protection, and statutory privacy overlays"}, {"id": "57bfb4af-e212-4d34-bf47-fb8f0fb7c439", "type": "concept", "title": "Platform moderation, algorithmic discrimination, and automated government decision-making"}], "description": "Students learn how older rights doctrines adapt to new capabilities."}, {"id": "186ca0f3-6dfa-459f-b784-c787a9ee5b7f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "National security and public health", "children": [{"id": "39aab9e2-1bab-40d2-9aec-0b399e67db6f", "type": "concept", "title": "War powers, detention, surveillance, immigration, material support, and state secrets"}, {"id": "2a6e9e90-e8bc-4635-8b9a-9c8547f27ac1", "type": "concept", "title": "Public health mandates, emergency orders, religious exemptions, due process, and equal protection"}, {"id": "271f5606-a478-4f1b-b22f-8ab85484bf67", "type": "concept", "title": "Deference, necessity, proportionality, sunset clauses, and accountability"}], "description": "Emergency governance is a recurring stress test for civil liberties."}, {"id": "981b00df-46d1-4544-8fe4-bba7ce0cf408", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Education, democracy, and contested public life", "children": [{"id": "bc693ff3-761c-4c46-b64f-454d578341a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Book bans, curriculum regulation, compelled speech, student discipline, and campus protest"}, {"id": "c6726f28-eb83-45e9-aea8-5fe1f43c870e", "type": "concept", "title": "Election administration, disinformation, voting access, and political retaliation"}, {"id": "569fa505-ccbd-4223-be23-025039dd8fa4", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil liberties in polarized communities and professional public-service settings"}], "description": "Rights disputes often concentrate in schools, elections, and civic institutions."}]}, {"id": "efd26885-419d-4d76-b2a2-f26714ad7a04", "type": "topic", "title": "Integrated Civil Liberties Capstone: Briefing, Moot Court, Policy, and Rights Strategy", "description": "Synthesize doctrine through a realistic rights dispute involving facts, litigation posture, institutional stakes, and public consequences.", "children": [{"id": "691c3ee3-ca40-4521-b65a-8d4a1259f95e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Case-file analysis", "children": [{"id": "c2dc64f6-345a-4c17-a02c-7c4c7fd0b779", "type": "concept", "title": "Client intake, government action, standing, state action, ripeness, and remedy choice"}, {"id": "5c6a8e2d-acae-484b-a8bc-733a401b905e", "type": "concept", "title": "Rights provisions, scrutiny, precedent map, factual record, and burden of proof"}, {"id": "6725374c-d9de-4b2a-9b9c-3f84385bd39a", "type": "concept", "title": "Litigation risks, public narrative, institutional constraints, and settlement possibilities"}], "description": "Students learn to turn facts into constitutional claims, defenses, and remedies."}, {"id": "40cc0b96-8a47-4561-a966-4d01ef1ed5b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Briefing and oral advocacy", "children": [{"id": "233fa497-bd49-4ca7-a720-be5d434204d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Question presented, rule synthesis, standard of review, argument headings, and record citations"}, {"id": "78387c54-a95f-4b76-a032-1b87dbf9ce28", "type": "concept", "title": "Responding to hypotheticals, concessions, limiting principles, and doctrinal consequences"}, {"id": "df102454-b278-4970-ab2a-02b8b6869ba4", "type": "concept", "title": "Moot court, judicial philosophy, institutional legitimacy, and advocacy ethics"}], "description": "Constitutional law demands precision, judgment, and persuasive use of precedent."}, {"id": "ea687edc-dde7-49bd-81e1-07313acd13c8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Policy and reform strategy", "children": [{"id": "2876c4d2-1b32-422e-85aa-e738dad42c77", "type": "concept", "title": "Legislative reform, administrative rules, consent decrees, compliance design, and public education"}, {"id": "4ff6ab94-7cf5-4a01-bad7-b55e835aac97", "type": "concept", "title": "Coalitions, community impact, media strategy, and implementation"}, {"id": "43ff8051-9fde-401d-b8cd-6407a04fbb7a", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparing constitutional litigation, statutory litigation, state constitutional claims, and policy advocacy"}], "description": "Not every civil-liberties problem is solved by courts alone."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$clcljson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'constitutional-law-civil-liberties'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Constitutional Law / Civil Liberties',
  goal = 'Constitutional Law / Civil Liberties',
  summary = 'This course studies American constitutional rights and civil liberties through Supreme Court doctrine, constitutional text, historical development, litigation strategy, and contemporary public-law disputes. Students examine due process, equal protection, speech, press, religion, criminal procedure, privacy, voting, state action, incorporation, federalism, remedies, and current civil-liberties conflicts involving technology, education, national security, public health, and democracy.',
  data = $clcljson${"slug": "constitutional-law-civil-liberties", "title": "Constitutional Law / Civil Liberties", "description": "This course studies American constitutional rights and civil liberties through Supreme Court doctrine, constitutional text, historical development, litigation strategy, and contemporary public-law disputes. Students examine due process, equal protection, speech, press, religion, criminal procedure, privacy, voting, state action, incorporation, federalism, remedies, and current civil-liberties conflicts involving technology, education, national security, public health, and democracy.", "topics": [{"id": "14f6f71a-8794-4e34-aea6-839ae6b260cf", "type": "topic", "title": "Constitutional Foundations, Civil Liberties, and Interpretive Method", "description": "Frame American civil liberties as rights claims made through text, history, doctrine, structure, precedent, institutions, and social movements.", "children": [{"id": "0a3ed85e-ac4f-4c7e-99a7-f96f8c77da37", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Constitutional text and rights architecture", "children": [{"id": "f5b023a0-d847-4129-bef1-342e66b6de5d", "type": "concept", "title": "Articles, Bill of Rights, Reconstruction Amendments, and later amendments"}, {"id": "69d86019-86ee-48d4-ae86-df95f9282661", "type": "concept", "title": "Negative liberties, equality guarantees, procedural protections, and structural safeguards"}, {"id": "560a1c61-1d8c-4e6e-8ba5-49fa1b1b08ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil liberties, civil rights, individual rights, group rights, and institutional power"}], "description": "Students learn where rights come from and how the Constitution organizes liberty claims."}, {"id": "74cda9af-73d6-408a-929d-38f4b99fde43", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modes of interpretation", "children": [{"id": "1b0b8075-929a-4485-b264-dd6a0b7dd907", "type": "concept", "title": "Textualism, originalism, living constitutionalism, common-law constitutionalism, and representation-reinforcement"}, {"id": "5052b9ac-5277-4dbc-956b-fbf63a875c80", "type": "concept", "title": "History and tradition, precedent, consequences, constitutional structure, and moral reasoning"}, {"id": "103ffa28-7aee-44b7-89d0-4d4cdf409b9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Majority, concurrence, dissent, dicta, holding, rule, standard, and test"}], "description": "Interpretive method shapes every disputed civil-liberties case."}, {"id": "a4e12423-6631-43f8-92e4-28227f2d8bb1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Civil liberties in American political development", "children": [{"id": "0784e88e-5b96-4eae-b570-f96330b3b217", "type": "concept", "title": "Slavery, Reconstruction, Jim Crow, incorporation, civil rights movements, and modern polarization"}, {"id": "c0bc396b-de2c-4716-8178-2ee00bb02ef2", "type": "concept", "title": "Federal courts, Congress, states, social movements, agencies, and public opinion"}, {"id": "05644d55-536a-4251-aa56-b21dd25a9226", "type": "concept", "title": "Rights expansion, backlash, enforcement gaps, and contested constitutional memory"}], "description": "Rights doctrine becomes clearer when students see it as part of history and power."}]}, {"id": "f01078b0-a2a4-4e3c-918e-d1d9be46ea5c", "type": "topic", "title": "Judicial Review, Justiciability, Remedies, and Constitutional Litigation", "description": "Teach how constitutional claims reach courts and what courts can actually do.", "children": [{"id": "666c0667-585b-40f4-94ec-1023b76a3ee6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Judicial review and constitutional supremacy", "children": [{"id": "322f4781-124b-4c2e-85b3-db6e82dbc054", "type": "concept", "title": "Marbury v. Madison, departmentalism, judicial supremacy, and constitutional dialogue"}, {"id": "b381d459-ca43-46b5-a887-8f67697c8335", "type": "concept", "title": "Supreme Court hierarchy, lower federal courts, state courts, and precedent"}, {"id": "5b68e619-fd90-4f9a-afef-2e1b4e94d205", "type": "concept", "title": "Countermajoritarian difficulty, legitimacy, and democratic accountability"}], "description": "Students learn why courts can invalidate government action and why that power is contested."}, {"id": "ef2c5099-203b-4ba8-84ce-2af763b476c0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Justiciability and access to courts", "children": [{"id": "00b42665-eefc-4231-a199-9ca5c7588222", "type": "concept", "title": "Standing, injury, causation, redressability, ripeness, mootness, political questions, and advisory opinions"}, {"id": "2f884f21-361b-464e-8bb2-9f71ffebfc53", "type": "concept", "title": "Third-party standing, taxpayer standing, organizational standing, and overbreadth exceptions"}, {"id": "9f52878c-96a2-464c-9f99-04e401d577c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Emergency docket, equitable discretion, class actions, and institutional plaintiffs"}], "description": "Rights depend on doctrines that decide who may sue, when, and over what injury."}, {"id": "7c069e33-763d-419f-bfcc-1740054905e3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Remedies and rights enforcement", "children": [{"id": "fcd4b30d-4ad9-4758-b36b-04abe35ebf23", "type": "concept", "title": "Injunctions, declaratory relief, damages, habeas, suppression, exclusion, and structural reform"}, {"id": "9ed3adf9-2ed5-4642-baec-f0b8141c841e", "type": "concept", "title": "Section 1983, Bivens, qualified immunity, sovereign immunity, and municipal liability"}, {"id": "0c34e5e2-5085-4286-a694-511414823c58", "type": "concept", "title": "Facial versus as-applied challenges, severability, nationwide injunctions, and remedial tailoring"}], "description": "Constitutional law is practical only if students understand remedies."}]}, {"id": "115bb4f5-fdd3-4a9a-b689-ba93cc274d05", "type": "topic", "title": "Federalism, Separation of Powers, State Action, and Incorporation Context", "description": "Give civil-liberties students the structural context that determines who is bound by constitutional rights.", "children": [{"id": "38b18c9a-0b2a-4732-b149-b78ef4a1916c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Federalism and rights", "children": [{"id": "b1778087-d7aa-4881-8968-fd313a7c3b0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Enumerated powers, Tenth Amendment, Supremacy Clause, preemption, and anti-commandeering"}, {"id": "45720bbd-a6a5-424f-907d-c3b3ce210f7a", "type": "concept", "title": "Fourteenth Amendment enforcement power and Reconstruction constitutionalism"}, {"id": "fc0b040c-bd94-4886-98f7-9efea42513c1", "type": "concept", "title": "State constitutions, state courts, local government, and rights laboratories"}], "description": "Federalism can protect liberty, frustrate rights enforcement, or create rights variation."}, {"id": "a1fd8792-586f-44b0-a4ef-7c821134b8fa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Separation of powers and emergencies", "children": [{"id": "79e375a3-c15a-42e1-a32f-c39dace2177b", "type": "concept", "title": "Congressional power, executive power, war powers, Youngstown framework, and administrative authority"}, {"id": "85b4f6a6-fd03-455e-ac5f-53b902bceb46", "type": "concept", "title": "National security, immigration, emergency powers, public health, and emergency litigation"}, {"id": "85f32474-146e-4ee9-ab8a-0b25d20aa7bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Judicial deference, political accountability, and civil-liberties risk"}], "description": "Rights disputes often arise when branches claim urgent power."}, {"id": "75427a08-d31c-4ed7-a852-e708da32ddaa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State action and incorporation", "children": [{"id": "6c4a34e4-f184-4147-882c-b26f1a5fcbe5", "type": "concept", "title": "State action doctrine, public function, entanglement, coercion, encouragement, and joint participation"}, {"id": "3f83e4e0-bd81-4f90-8d39-2bedcebe3fa5", "type": "concept", "title": "Fourteenth Amendment incorporation, selective incorporation, and ordered liberty"}, {"id": "2f7d60ba-57c6-4382-ae20-2ab9609f16d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil Rights Cases, Shelley, public accommodations, platforms, and privatized governance"}], "description": "Students learn when private conduct becomes constitutional conduct and which rights bind states."}]}, {"id": "ea8609cd-75b2-41d2-abc6-20690b9e8b0b", "type": "topic", "title": "Bill of Rights Incorporation and Ordered Liberty", "description": "Study how rights first written against the federal government became enforceable against states.", "children": [{"id": "930eb5e2-2b49-4121-aa9a-ef7a51e3e67a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Incorporation doctrine", "children": [{"id": "59907518-1ae0-4662-b225-abf41e899ceb", "type": "concept", "title": "Privileges or Immunities Clause, Due Process Clause, and selective incorporation"}, {"id": "7759bf2e-0ee7-40ad-80f8-6f9e1118115b", "type": "concept", "title": "Fundamental-to-ordered-liberty test and deeply rooted rights"}, {"id": "bf151023-593c-41bc-83e3-258ef7819f40", "type": "concept", "title": "McDonald, Timbs, jury rights, criminal procedure, and remaining unincorporated rights"}], "description": "Incorporation explains why most rights litigation is against state and local government."}, {"id": "bcfc2bfa-0c36-456c-bb0e-f1650298c524", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ordered liberty and rights hierarchy", "children": [{"id": "71c4cf51-5b2e-4ce0-9613-8c76773c9165", "type": "concept", "title": "Fundamental rights, preferred freedoms, rational basis, intermediate scrutiny, strict scrutiny, and history-and-tradition tests"}, {"id": "7bc8d8c8-cb7b-44cc-81d1-8a315536fed9", "type": "concept", "title": "Rights balancing, categorical rules, tiers of scrutiny, and administrability"}, {"id": "96c55df1-9f13-4988-8b2b-9c1bc60ba0d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional competence, judicial restraint, and judicial engagement"}], "description": "Students learn that not all rights receive the same doctrinal treatment."}, {"id": "4bb24861-3e8f-4e9e-82b4-953e5984b0d8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rights against states and local officials", "children": [{"id": "99094d13-3830-48e9-a384-d2eee20de31c", "type": "concept", "title": "Municipal ordinances, school boards, police departments, prisons, and licensing bodies"}, {"id": "78a092b3-5fd2-4779-82e0-6149a50c5b3c", "type": "concept", "title": "State constitutional analogues and independent state grounds"}, {"id": "edb3affd-eeac-4743-8165-5b97ec9ddeae", "type": "concept", "title": "Federalism, uniformity, and local democratic experimentation"}], "description": "Incorporation doctrine becomes concrete through police, schools, prisons, zoning, and local regulation."}]}, {"id": "f5b94a2e-98f6-467b-a2bc-a555ab723b88", "type": "topic", "title": "Procedural Due Process, Fair Procedures, and Government Deprivation", "description": "Learn how the Constitution regulates government procedures before life, liberty, or property is taken.", "children": [{"id": "b1d7d5e0-0a00-477a-86d6-ee8da281b842", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Protected interests", "children": [{"id": "d3714813-e38d-4a77-8acd-76647c97ca2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Property interests, entitlement theory, licenses, employment, benefits, and education"}, {"id": "c231e9ab-8ab5-400a-b81d-d96f115612b1", "type": "concept", "title": "Liberty interests, stigma-plus, physical restraint, reputation, and family integrity"}, {"id": "4bd1e103-7ac0-4cf7-a75b-e43c2ba7ff9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Prison, immigration, parole, discipline, and administrative deprivations"}], "description": "Due process begins by identifying whether life, liberty, or property is at stake."}, {"id": "a8baa979-e3f4-4a88-ab7d-8162ae973063", "type": "subtopic", "title": "What process is due", "children": [{"id": "4f016489-b505-42ab-8740-92113895c7bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Mathews v. Eldridge factors: private interest, risk of error, value of safeguards, and government burden"}, {"id": "a8b439f6-33bd-419b-99f7-0ac1b6846618", "type": "concept", "title": "Notice, hearing, neutral decisionmaker, counsel, evidence, confrontation, and written reasons"}, {"id": "47810fc4-4251-46c2-a2f0-b634f085e0db", "type": "concept", "title": "Pre-deprivation versus post-deprivation process and emergency exceptions"}], "description": "Students learn the balancing framework for procedural fairness."}, {"id": "f4acbcf6-986d-4b9e-9eb0-bb9bb47107e7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Administrative and institutional settings", "children": [{"id": "81e25abb-597d-435d-838b-01d26cca1e5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Schools, welfare, disability benefits, public employment, licensing, housing, and civil commitment"}, {"id": "b6e7866f-950d-48bc-b80e-b52706537e0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Campus discipline, immigration proceedings, algorithmic decision-making, and automated benefits"}, {"id": "78ce87aa-61da-4a6f-aafc-1621359d8fa1", "type": "concept", "title": "Remedy design, harmless error, exhaustion, and judicial review"}], "description": "Procedural due process is a practical field of hearings, records, and institutional design."}]}, {"id": "3414a96b-7018-43fe-88e1-15c9f28e98b2", "type": "topic", "title": "Substantive Due Process, Privacy, Family, and Bodily Autonomy", "description": "Study the contested doctrine protecting certain liberties from government interference regardless of procedure.", "children": [{"id": "174a399c-a2f0-4c5e-bb1d-eb67e5277264", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fundamental liberty framework", "children": [{"id": "09ba62f0-becb-4030-a20b-b32668e27c57", "type": "concept", "title": "Deeply rooted tradition, ordered liberty, careful description, and history-and-tradition methodology"}, {"id": "70b4db9d-ba29-4618-9b48-97c239f5ebce", "type": "concept", "title": "Strict scrutiny, rational basis, undue burden, and doctrinal instability"}, {"id": "3a20896f-8428-4379-bb04-fdb1ca7699de", "type": "concept", "title": "Lochner legacy, judicial restraint, and contested legitimacy"}], "description": "Students learn how courts identify and protect unenumerated rights."}, {"id": "91f2bfce-56ef-4413-8fda-1dbc870875fb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Family, marriage, sexuality, and identity", "children": [{"id": "b1414bfc-18d5-4452-9b58-f7086a0ca8cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage, contraception, parenting, family integrity, custody, and education"}, {"id": "902d823c-12ff-4ae0-91cd-78089f200e8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Sexual intimacy, LGBTQ rights, Obergefell, Lawrence, and equal-protection overlap"}, {"id": "9fef57a8-b38c-4267-acf6-d1d0074a8465", "type": "concept", "title": "Adoption, foster care, trans rights, and parental-rights disputes"}], "description": "Substantive due process has shaped intimate life and family governance."}, {"id": "569dd222-49d4-49a2-8140-af33f78d9d08", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Bodily autonomy and reproductive liberty", "children": [{"id": "900c842f-0c73-41f6-84f2-4cb4219120ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Abortion doctrine before and after Dobbs, state constitutional rights, and interstate conflict"}, {"id": "aed8ecbe-5651-4ff4-815a-556399aeb4bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Contraception, pregnancy, medical decision-making, refusal of treatment, and assisted reproduction"}, {"id": "7d671f71-7403-44e7-b7af-79532facb15d", "type": "concept", "title": "Public health, vaccination, bodily integrity, disability, and bioethics"}], "description": "Students confront one of the most contested constitutional fields."}]}, {"id": "7579daa2-f90f-4a46-a615-5859e850a618", "type": "topic", "title": "Equal Protection Framework and Levels of Scrutiny", "description": "Build the analytic core for classification, discrimination, and equality claims.", "children": [{"id": "65a74c59-0da7-4e04-9024-3362decad89b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Equal protection basics", "children": [{"id": "142634fc-a886-42fa-8026-e9fda49a1c44", "type": "concept", "title": "Facial classifications, discriminatory purpose, discriminatory effect, underinclusion, and overinclusion"}, {"id": "2edb9e53-4e56-4083-b92c-8364771e2d9b", "type": "concept", "title": "Rational basis, rational basis with bite, intermediate scrutiny, and strict scrutiny"}, {"id": "3d44205c-508f-453a-97c2-4eb37ec9f64a", "type": "concept", "title": "Fit, tailoring, compelling interests, important interests, and legitimate interests"}], "description": "Students learn how courts identify classifications and choose scrutiny."}, {"id": "2749d137-ec3e-43d9-b036-bfbb351210a0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intent, impact, and proof", "children": [{"id": "042572c2-4b8f-4608-8f21-beba0c7e861a", "type": "concept", "title": "Washington v. Davis, Arlington Heights factors, statistical evidence, history, departures from normal process, and sequence of events"}, {"id": "1e4db15f-e242-487c-b8ff-51dcaed264b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Implicit bias, structural inequality, disparate impact statutes, and constitutional limits"}, {"id": "0f2f4284-92b1-47fc-8f81-833277af825c", "type": "concept", "title": "Burden shifting, comparators, animus, and evidentiary inference"}], "description": "Equality doctrine often turns on evidence of motive and institutional context."}, {"id": "c73667ea-1b9b-4622-aad2-632641d762f2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Remedies and equality enforcement", "children": [{"id": "749da64a-3009-4273-bd79-fa6b3c5cf1cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Injunctions, race-conscious remedies, desegregation orders, consent decrees, and monitoring"}, {"id": "aa2709ae-0567-460a-9825-a62aa2ac1355", "type": "concept", "title": "Leveling up versus leveling down"}, {"id": "1d790a9a-66b7-498c-b03b-fb52fcde4f8e", "type": "concept", "title": "Section 5 enforcement, Title VI, Title VII, Section 1983, and state-law complements"}], "description": "Students learn that equal protection remedies are both doctrinal and institutional."}]}, {"id": "d0376519-1096-4d49-af75-76e8a8725a4d", "type": "topic", "title": "Race, Ethnicity, Voting Rights, and Democratic Equality", "description": "Center race and democratic participation as core constitutional equality fields.", "children": [{"id": "0ae2987f-4c0a-488d-bb57-1407672366a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race and suspect classifications", "children": [{"id": "8da441b9-81d4-4087-898e-bfa3c22aca66", "type": "concept", "title": "Dred Scott, Reconstruction Amendments, Plessy, Brown, desegregation, and modern strict scrutiny"}, {"id": "22a2f232-bfc0-432c-b8c0-e4f08f64d5eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Benign versus invidious classifications, affirmative action, diversity rationales, and remedial interests"}, {"id": "882040f8-7d9f-44c0-bfae-0f76e5718bc1", "type": "concept", "title": "Education, contracting, employment, admissions, and government programs"}], "description": "Students learn why racial classifications receive strict scrutiny and how doctrine treats racial purpose."}, {"id": "4187ddd1-7237-447a-b644-b5d924831034", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Voting rights and representation", "children": [{"id": "396e99ef-a7fe-4a97-954a-2a2f67e32247", "type": "concept", "title": "One person one vote, reapportionment, ballot access, political parties, and election administration"}, {"id": "030d498a-a9a0-4a2d-9f63-4de7b1b3b458", "type": "concept", "title": "Racial gerrymandering, partisan gerrymandering, vote dilution, and Voting Rights Act context"}, {"id": "cee58e12-3e9c-47f4-bd04-77584d0d43b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Mail voting, voter ID, felon disenfranchisement, campaign administration, and emergency election disputes"}], "description": "Political equality is a constitutional and statutory field."}, {"id": "c1ce7888-0af5-4d09-b1d2-9845bc13a9bf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Citizenship, immigration, and belonging", "children": [{"id": "e23946f1-08d0-4e60-a575-c0a8931e6185", "type": "concept", "title": "Alienage classifications, undocumented persons, birthright citizenship, and immigration federalism"}, {"id": "c55e71e7-ab24-48ef-9c20-fa96e748a4d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Language access, tribal sovereignty, equal protection, and plenary power"}, {"id": "78fa7e06-061f-4ca9-a26b-a6fb0a87e7ef", "type": "concept", "title": "National security, exclusion, detention, and constitutional limits at the border"}], "description": "Race and ethnicity intersect with citizenship, alienage, and national membership."}]}, {"id": "fc368417-49b5-4b46-9441-62e9d5a63b18", "type": "topic", "title": "Sex, Gender, Sexual Orientation, Disability, and Emerging Equality Claims", "description": "Extend equality doctrine beyond race while showing how categories and scrutiny evolve.", "children": [{"id": "36f7036d-b085-4123-87f7-715dab808f5a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sex and gender classifications", "children": [{"id": "39d96368-cd5c-489a-9cb5-67cd3791e5f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Frontiero, Craig, Virginia, stereotypes, pregnancy, caregiving, and sex-based generalizations"}, {"id": "158233b5-1bb5-4c93-8592-41a868482ad8", "type": "concept", "title": "Biological difference, military service, education, athletics, and public facilities"}, {"id": "fbe71a9c-94ce-4569-9392-c50e7e6fa61c", "type": "concept", "title": "Equal Rights Amendment history and state constitutional equality provisions"}], "description": "Students learn intermediate scrutiny and its modern applications."}, {"id": "807d4c42-377c-460f-ba3a-f997b8c15f71", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sexual orientation and gender identity", "children": [{"id": "31144485-ba28-4c6c-8726-a64787ca7266", "type": "concept", "title": "Romer, Lawrence, Windsor, Obergefell, Bostock context, and animus doctrine"}, {"id": "68d5049b-145b-4158-8977-ba537ff48d23", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage, adoption, public accommodations, employment, education, and healthcare"}, {"id": "85cb475d-6be2-4632-b60c-f611c5aa6e9f", "type": "concept", "title": "Transgender rights, youth healthcare, sports, speech, religion, and parental claims"}], "description": "Students connect equal protection, due process, statutory rights, and contemporary disputes."}, {"id": "8bd6494d-2edc-4ba7-a333-3165050f4b91", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Disability, poverty, age, and other classifications", "children": [{"id": "d8a035e8-9188-4de3-97dd-771a65035514", "type": "concept", "title": "Disability, age, wealth, education, homelessness, and criminal history"}, {"id": "ba95adee-52a6-4636-b221-d150e81f4fbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Rational basis review, statutory civil-rights overlays, and access-to-justice concerns"}, {"id": "225ee735-8e97-44fe-9a25-0776ecb8f051", "type": "concept", "title": "Equal protection versus due process, administrative burdens, and social citizenship"}], "description": "Not all important classifications receive heightened scrutiny, which raises hard policy questions."}]}, {"id": "0e2102b2-b5db-4131-99c2-e4401e6237d8", "type": "topic", "title": "First Amendment Speech Foundations", "description": "Study the core rules protecting expression from government control.", "children": [{"id": "a2a9cbc0-a424-42be-a06e-e164fd62096e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Why speech is protected", "children": [{"id": "3d5c6be3-5d11-43e0-852e-6f4c3d3c9e9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Democratic self-government, marketplace of ideas, autonomy, truth seeking, and checking function"}, {"id": "99553f1c-04a4-4228-bdf7-7a011762edf8", "type": "concept", "title": "Speech, expression, conduct, association, press, and petition"}, {"id": "59011e17-bdc7-4366-b800-dd6470078979", "type": "concept", "title": "State action, government regulation, private platforms, and public values"}], "description": "Students learn the theories that explain strong protection for expression."}, {"id": "623e84ed-3942-47f3-9c02-eadf13daa2ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Content and viewpoint discrimination", "children": [{"id": "1b4606b5-1de8-449c-9a33-6613ab2f6dae", "type": "concept", "title": "Content-based regulation, viewpoint discrimination, strict scrutiny, and facial neutrality"}, {"id": "433676d2-7b3f-4331-b934-8907b5d76f53", "type": "concept", "title": "Speaker-based distinctions, subject-matter distinctions, compelled speech, and editorial discretion"}, {"id": "23095203-b58d-4ba0-95cc-3e19d1594797", "type": "concept", "title": "Overbreadth, vagueness, prior restraints, and chilling effects"}], "description": "The central First Amendment danger is government control over ideas."}, {"id": "0b683bab-ff88-4799-ad82-725546817537", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Unprotected and less-protected speech", "children": [{"id": "82b34bb3-c7b3-42b3-ab80-d784e542e67e", "type": "concept", "title": "Incitement, true threats, fighting words, obscenity, defamation, fraud, and child sexual abuse material"}, {"id": "5014265a-0702-47ba-a29c-bcba0a42dc18", "type": "concept", "title": "Commercial speech, professional speech, campaign finance, and government-subsidized speech"}, {"id": "90eb7916-cc56-4a06-b17a-59b35f26f25d", "type": "concept", "title": "Categorical rules, historical tests, and balancing approaches"}], "description": "Students learn the narrow categories where speech may be regulated more directly."}]}, {"id": "e86db01c-7a1b-4328-8984-c80acb67451b", "type": "topic", "title": "Speech Regulation: Forums, Time-Place-Manner, Schools, Government Speech, and Platforms", "description": "Apply speech doctrine to public spaces, institutions, and modern communication systems.", "children": [{"id": "5b149e36-62b9-48a8-ab29-f9998e23aa27", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public forum doctrine", "children": [{"id": "237fcc16-ec42-4183-a218-64c00e19afcb", "type": "concept", "title": "Traditional public forums, designated forums, limited forums, and nonpublic forums"}, {"id": "04278861-6441-407e-9b95-e13773556e27", "type": "concept", "title": "Time, place, and manner restrictions, alternative channels, narrow tailoring, and significant interests"}, {"id": "b15e6861-911b-4473-9a97-8cc3d13b2186", "type": "concept", "title": "Permits, protests, sidewalks, parks, campuses, airports, and courthouses"}], "description": "Forum analysis determines how much government can regulate speech on government property."}, {"id": "cac2cce9-0b56-4a53-857d-3289dbe13736", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Institutional speech contexts", "children": [{"id": "72898ffd-309e-41e7-9eaf-9033941fd02d", "type": "concept", "title": "Student speech, curricular speech, school discipline, and campus speech"}, {"id": "15f52633-915d-4a7d-b183-b56d1137ddda", "type": "concept", "title": "Public employees, government contractors, public universities, and academic freedom"}, {"id": "da855ff4-629e-494b-81b2-27af8cfbfbfd", "type": "concept", "title": "Prisons, military, public benefits, and unconstitutional conditions"}], "description": "Speech rights shift across schools, workplaces, prisons, military settings, and government programs."}, {"id": "a69d2ea0-d27b-40a5-90f3-353d0a059afd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital speech and platform governance", "children": [{"id": "0591bf57-664c-443c-92e9-18cf033db707", "type": "concept", "title": "Social media, content moderation, common-carrier theories, jawboning, and state platform laws"}, {"id": "0bac231a-149c-4ac8-b752-8e779c7bc549", "type": "concept", "title": "Government accounts, blocking users, public-official speech, and state action online"}, {"id": "1fc2a083-5f53-43e0-8c37-60edb1a81d6f", "type": "concept", "title": "Algorithms, misinformation, harassment, anonymity, and digital public squares"}], "description": "Modern civil liberties require serious attention to online speech."}]}, {"id": "51b56560-d619-4613-8737-9b84d657fec7", "type": "topic", "title": "Press, Assembly, Association, Petition, and Campaign Finance", "description": "Cover the expressive freedoms that organize democratic participation beyond individual speech.", "children": [{"id": "ce2c3578-7f95-466c-90a4-a22e6056c07d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Freedom of the press and newsgathering", "children": [{"id": "102ec05e-0fcb-4be4-8632-ea4350863c1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Prior restraints, publication of truthful information, leaks, national security, and reporter privilege"}, {"id": "1670a747-fee1-4603-95a1-d426972511a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Defamation, public figures, actual malice, privacy torts, and anti-SLAPP context"}, {"id": "391f8a43-2948-45a5-bfca-f9e9ba0f2f44", "type": "concept", "title": "Press access, recording police, gag orders, and digital journalism"}], "description": "Press doctrine protects publication but leaves hard questions about access and institutional privilege."}, {"id": "dd561b13-6a0e-4f02-afd4-d98455143dd9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Assembly, association, and petition", "children": [{"id": "3f33d400-9055-4391-9212-ff0cfd335a2a", "type": "concept", "title": "NAACP v. Alabama, expressive association, intimate association, compelled association, and disclosure rules"}, {"id": "f933c650-64ab-4c45-bb1f-bde5f193bbb0", "type": "concept", "title": "Protests, marches, boycotts, labor picketing, public safety, and permit systems"}, {"id": "4d95981a-abbe-4061-ba9d-0644017e8921", "type": "concept", "title": "Petition Clause, lobbying, litigation as expression, and retaliation"}], "description": "Collective expression is central to civil-rights movements and democratic contestation."}, {"id": "d337333a-a11d-4e68-ba6e-3e6894e7b8da", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Campaign finance and political participation", "children": [{"id": "95b9578c-c2ed-4a48-9ff7-970ac8b5e882", "type": "concept", "title": "Contribution limits, expenditure limits, independent expenditures, PACs, Super PACs, and disclosure"}, {"id": "c9aac3c9-23cc-46bc-aef9-d3a61bdd3092", "type": "concept", "title": "Buckley, Citizens United, McCutcheon, coordination, public financing, and corruption rationales"}, {"id": "ea853ab6-87df-4966-8c36-224fa876128b", "type": "concept", "title": "Election integrity, foreign influence, digital ads, and democratic equality"}], "description": "Campaign finance doctrine treats money, speech, equality, and corruption as constitutional problems."}]}, {"id": "4e34341a-f63c-4ef9-b41b-caf0cd2a92f3", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion Clauses: Establishment, Free Exercise, and Religious Liberty Conflicts", "description": "Study the constitutional management of religion, state neutrality, and pluralism.", "children": [{"id": "ffa3310b-6a2f-4283-b1b1-670b9018139b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Establishment Clause", "children": [{"id": "43dba36a-1ee1-4397-9398-167126701717", "type": "concept", "title": "Prayer, religious symbols, public funding, schools, monuments, chaplains, and coercion"}, {"id": "810e61e6-2930-4eed-9954-b1aa63784dce", "type": "concept", "title": "Lemon, endorsement, coercion, history-and-tradition, and neutrality"}, {"id": "6b32f84f-b50c-4f8c-8d41-6ad0b5c8fc00", "type": "concept", "title": "Government speech, private speech, and religious expression on public property"}], "description": "Students learn how courts police government support, endorsement, coercion, and historical practices."}, {"id": "257bf0d4-00d8-428b-be26-42e564baf4c5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Free Exercise Clause", "children": [{"id": "2b0fedaf-e520-41b1-aedf-791b25de6875", "type": "concept", "title": "Belief versus conduct, neutral laws of general applicability, Smith, Lukumi, and strict scrutiny"}, {"id": "fd63c03f-d5bd-471b-b1b3-c525a5bd5a89", "type": "concept", "title": "Hybrid rights, individualized exemptions, ministerial exception, and church autonomy"}, {"id": "4c51c6ec-ed73-45d8-9978-5e2cd47cf754", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious schools, employment, prisons, land use, and pandemic restrictions"}], "description": "Free exercise doctrine tests when religious conduct receives constitutional exemption."}, {"id": "e267ce9d-abad-4fe8-9a46-4561d4852d28", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion and equality conflicts", "children": [{"id": "6b42a6c8-2dfb-4d79-be30-b91ae7f5e079", "type": "concept", "title": "Public accommodations, wedding vendors, foster care, employment, and healthcare mandates"}, {"id": "dad576c1-b275-4091-b056-9f2ff9821db6", "type": "concept", "title": "RFRA, RLUIPA, state RFRAs, and statutory exemptions"}, {"id": "2fd3c22a-75e3-434c-9d4e-f0fcf3be1a09", "type": "concept", "title": "Pluralism, dignitary harms, conscience, and institutional settlement"}], "description": "Modern cases often pit religious liberty against antidiscrimination, healthcare, education, and speech claims."}]}, {"id": "a3653864-2bfd-46e2-9346-d526a1611014", "type": "topic", "title": "Criminal Procedure as Civil Liberties: Fourth, Fifth, Sixth, and Eighth Amendments", "description": "Connect civil liberties to policing, prosecution, punishment, and criminal justice institutions.", "children": [{"id": "eccacf1a-ede3-42e1-aa02-ae03ac826b8d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fourth Amendment searches and seizures", "children": [{"id": "485a7e65-6608-41a0-ade0-8e223eca5219", "type": "concept", "title": "Search, seizure, reasonable expectation of privacy, property-based tests, and standing"}, {"id": "baccf6f6-b985-43aa-b4ee-3825a377f9e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Warrants, probable cause, exceptions, stops, frisks, arrests, and reasonableness"}, {"id": "b1e0496e-e6d1-48e2-af23-e8969b3ccc14", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital searches, cell phones, location tracking, drones, databases, and surveillance"}], "description": "Students learn the constitutional baseline for policing and privacy."}, {"id": "1b6ddc8c-7286-4904-88ee-5c3d7e4ecaaf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fifth and Sixth Amendment protections", "children": [{"id": "8d4caf6b-0df8-4d03-9444-454f3d088df9", "type": "concept", "title": "Self-incrimination, Miranda, interrogation, due process voluntariness, and immunity"}, {"id": "12f7443e-4386-41be-98da-56139da83a56", "type": "concept", "title": "Right to counsel, confrontation, compulsory process, jury trial, and speedy trial"}, {"id": "afc49cf9-76f3-4b3e-87a4-de738754d091", "type": "concept", "title": "Plea bargaining, discovery, Brady, effective assistance, and wrongful convictions"}], "description": "Criminal process rights protect against coercion and unfair adjudication."}, {"id": "f230aa31-0ccb-42e5-8eec-e7f77cde4dc0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Eighth Amendment and punishment", "children": [{"id": "a5c75161-ba16-4a90-938a-3b720dba18bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Cruel and unusual punishment, proportionality, death penalty, juvenile sentencing, and prison conditions"}, {"id": "4c8f4b36-e386-485b-94e5-44b4643e3e24", "type": "concept", "title": "Excessive fines, bail, pretrial detention, homelessness enforcement, and civil forfeiture"}, {"id": "1709df72-e0c4-45f1-afdc-0b7d2e833234", "type": "concept", "title": "Qualified immunity, prison litigation, and remedial barriers"}], "description": "Punishment doctrine reveals the Constitution's limits on state violence."}]}, {"id": "8a9d6301-2844-405b-b07d-57ad5eb4c939", "type": "topic", "title": "Second Amendment, Takings, Economic Liberties, and Property-Adjacent Rights", "description": "Treat the rights often connected to public safety, property, markets, and economic regulation.", "children": [{"id": "09e344a9-f859-4ba5-8d7b-11fc0e0ec646", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Second Amendment", "children": [{"id": "06fe1fb7-3e49-498a-8b67-b5a6ab38eeec", "type": "concept", "title": "Heller, McDonald, Bruen, Rahimi, text-history-tradition, and historical analogues"}, {"id": "1747aca8-29f3-4182-aeef-b3910e21771d", "type": "concept", "title": "Public carry, sensitive places, licensing, domestic violence, felons, age restrictions, and assault weapons"}, {"id": "c64b19dd-9316-49c2-8fbf-54d8914cf5b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Federalism, public safety, empirical evidence, and historical method"}], "description": "Students learn the modern shift from militia framing to individual public-carry doctrine."}, {"id": "e0a1676c-1bc6-47c1-a2cd-24307c41f83e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Takings and property rights", "children": [{"id": "f7aa5535-bb06-449b-b85c-498b47c4aa4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Public use, just compensation, physical takings, regulatory takings, and exactions"}, {"id": "72bdbc3c-fc81-422f-85c0-d94ee1bd2cc5", "type": "concept", "title": "Kelo, Loretto, Lucas, Penn Central, Nollan, Dolan, Koontz, and Sheetz"}, {"id": "e4ee6cb8-962a-4a81-ab8a-211be523cd69", "type": "concept", "title": "Housing, land use, environmental regulation, and state constitutional property"}], "description": "Takings doctrine is both constitutional structure and civil-liberties limit on government regulation."}, {"id": "ace0a7e7-d9d7-4f00-a9b1-e821ed1f4c2a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Economic liberties and rational basis", "children": [{"id": "429d7e15-1e4a-4db4-9f28-3caf6dfb6a15", "type": "concept", "title": "Lochner, Nebbia, West Coast Hotel, Carolene Products, and modern rational basis"}, {"id": "1a326bec-3d71-4fd8-adf9-5450b2458261", "type": "concept", "title": "Occupational licensing, contracts, substantive due process, and privileges or immunities"}, {"id": "bc5d4ca3-7ea5-4aa9-b4aa-262189eaa446", "type": "concept", "title": "Economic regulation, inequality, administrative state, and judicial role"}], "description": "Students understand why economic rights moved from Lochner-era protection to deferential review."}]}, {"id": "6dcf65c4-33e5-4a34-a747-08232fd1babb", "type": "topic", "title": "Civil Rights Enforcement, Section 1983, Federal Courts, and Institutional Reform", "description": "Show how rights become enforceable against officials, institutions, and governments.", "children": [{"id": "3874d74e-a0f3-4f08-94b0-de32f629cb80", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Section 1983 litigation", "children": [{"id": "286a0174-4e41-4f91-bf03-3fe45c10b67b", "type": "concept", "title": "Color of law, person, deprivation, causation, municipal liability, and official policy"}, {"id": "6722807f-ef79-457d-814c-b77b6e2b695c", "type": "concept", "title": "Qualified immunity, clearly established law, supervisory liability, and individual-capacity claims"}, {"id": "97b53eaa-f43c-4273-8739-95ec76df4aa5", "type": "concept", "title": "Damages, attorneys' fees, settlement, and litigation strategy"}], "description": "Section 1983 is the practical engine of constitutional civil-rights enforcement